{
 "schema": 1,
 "slug": "captured-device",
 "title": "A radio is captured",
 "question": "If someone steals one of our radios, how fast can we cut it off \u2014 and can an outsider get in at all?",
 "headlines": [
  {
   "value": "0.3 s",
   "label": "to shut a captured radio out of the whole mesh",
   "detail": "median over 10 runs, revocation pushed to HQ alone; worst 0.4 s"
  },
  {
   "value": "0",
   "label": "outsider packets accepted",
   "detail": "of 2100 sent by three would-be intruders"
  },
  {
   "value": "0.30 s",
   "label": "for a legitimate new radio to join",
   "detail": "from power-on, with no operator involvement"
  }
 ],
 "summary": "A captured radio is the hardest case. It holds a valid certificate and the matching key, so to any cryptographic check it is a member. The operator revokes it by pushing one signed record to the HQ gateway, and the mesh carries that record the rest of the way. In the run charted here, all 11 other radios held the revocation 0.25 s after the push. From then on none of them admitted or routed to the captured radio, and HQ accepted 0 of its packets. The field team's traffic had been relayed through the captured radio. It moved to another path, losing 1 packet in the 10 s after the push. Meanwhile three outsiders streamed at HQ for the whole run: an open router, a node with a flawless certificate from another mesh, and a lapsed member. None of their packets were accepted. A genuinely new member's traffic was accepted 0.30 s after it switched on.",
